# Authenticated macOS display recovery

Failure: the Host can remain discoverable after its selected virtual display
becomes inactive. The Client reads topology before posting display preparation,
so an unavailable topology blocks the path that could restore the display.

Keep topology snapshots read-only and exact. Add one explicit recovery hook for
an authenticated topology request, rechecking peer-bound authorization and
graphical ownership before/during/after work. Public discovery, app-list reads,
invalid tokens and active streams must not trigger display mutation. The
graphical main queue owns all display work; only the existing auth lane waits.
Request expiry/cancellation and a three-second recovery deadline stop work.

Recover only the same agent-owned virtual display, retaining its last successful
mode and encoding profile. Declare remote user activity through IOKit, release
the temporary assertion on every outcome, republish the existing modes if
offline, and validate real active geometry. Never fabricate a topology, reset
TCC, change physical display modes, create duplicate outputs, reboot, or alter
persistent power settings. Failure remains a bounded 503, not false success.

Validation: synthetic CG/IOKit tests for healthy no-op, inactive/online and
offline restoration, 5K mode retention, no unowned-display recovery, revoked
authority, timeout, concurrent rejection and assertion cleanup. Real loopback
TLS tests for unauthenticated/no-op paths, successful recovery, failed recovery,
revocation and request expiry with responsive public discovery. Require hosted
macOS compilation. Live sleeping/inactive-output recovery, login/logout and
ownership-transition checks remain hardware gates before acceptance.

Reference: Apple's
[IOPMAssertionDeclareUserActivity](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/iokit/1557127-iopmassertiondeclareuseractivity)
documents display wake/user activity without requiring special privileges.
This is not evidence that sleep caused a particular inactive-display incident.
